<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Classroom News – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Will Write the Article About Our School Trip.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe plans for upcoming events. interactive: Clarify personal opinions in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe plans for the newsletter content (e.g., 'Tomorrow, we will go on a school trip.'). Can share opinions on others' newsletter ideas and ask follow-up questions (e.g., 'This is a great article. Did you like the topic?'). Can clarify opinions in group discussions about the classroom newsletter (e.g., 'What do you think about adding more photos?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays a large picture of a class on a school trip. The teacher asks, "Who remembers our last school trip?" Students quickly share one or two words. Then, the teacher says, "Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the Emberá Community. It was exciting and fun! What did you like?" Students quickly tell a partner one thing they liked. (Activating Past Simple)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a blank 'Classroom Newsletter' template on the board. "We need to make our classroom newsletter about the school trip! I will write the headline. What will I write?" (Teacher writes an example headline). "And I will collect pictures for the newsletter. What will I do?" (Teacher mimes collecting photos). The teacher emphasizes the main verb in contractions: "I'll <u>write</u> the headline," "I'll <u>collect</u> pictures."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I talking about yesterday or tomorrow?" (Expected answer: "Tomorrow/Future")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I sharing a plan or something I did already?" (Expected answer: "A plan")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two students (Sara and Tomás) planning content for the classroom newsletter.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Dialogue Snippet:**</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Sara: "Hi Tomás! We need ideas for the next newsletter. What will you do?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Tomás: "I will write an article about our school trip. Yesterday, we visited the Emberá Community. It was so exciting!"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Sara: "That’s a great idea for a topic! I will take pictures. What else do we need?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Tomás: "We need an announcement for the next school event. And we'll need a good layout. This column will be for photos."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Sara: "Yes! And I'll describe the art project we did. Did you like the topic?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Oral comprehension of short, simple dialogues related to familiar contexts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Teacher reads dialogue twice):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "What are Sara and Tomás planning?" (Expected answer: "The classroom newsletter.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "Tomás will collect pictures." (Expected answer: "False. Sara will.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: "Who will write an article about the school trip?" (Expected answer: "Tomás.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice describing plans using future tense (will/won't), asking and answering questions in Present Simple and Past Simple, and using target vocabulary (article, newsletter, pictures, event, topic, write, take, share, discuss, creative). Pronunciation practice for emphasizing verbs in 'I'll' and 'we'll'. Phonemic awareness: identifying short /ɑ/ in 'art' and long /oʊ/ in 'show'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Individual to Pair):</b> Teacher provides sentence starters on the board related to the newsletter. Students complete them orally.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"For our classroom newsletter, I will _______ (e.g., write an article)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We need _______ (e.g., more pictures for the event)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Yesterday, I _______ (e.g., took notes about the trip)."</li>
                    </ul>
                    Students then turn to a partner and say their completed sentences. Teacher monitors pronunciation, specifically emphasizing the main verb in future contractions ('I'll <u>write</u>').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Q&A Chain Drill (Whole Class):</b> Teacher starts by asking a student, "What will you do for our newsletter?" The student answers using 'I will...' and then asks the next student, "What about you, [Name]? What will you do?" Or "What else do we need?" The teacher guides students to incorporate vocabulary like 'article', 'pictures', 'topic', 'announcement', 'event', 'discuss', 'organize'. Teacher can also prompt with past simple questions: "When did we go on the school trip?" "What did we see?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Newsletter Planning Interview":</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A asks Partner B questions about their ideas for the classroom newsletter, and Partner B responds. Then they switch roles.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will you write about for the newsletter?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Will you take photos or draw pictures?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What topic do you think is most exciting?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When did the school trip happen?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What else do we need to make it colorful?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    Pairs are encouraged to ask follow-up questions like, "Did you like the topic?" or "Why is it meaningful?" Teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and prompting for more elaborate responses.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Discussion and Planning. Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to plan their section of "Our Classroom News" about the school trip. Each group acts as a mini-editorial team.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher assigns each group a specific section of the newsletter (e.g., 'School Trip Highlights,' 'Student Opinions,' 'Upcoming Events').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and decides:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What articles/topics will they include? (e.g., "We will write an article about the fun activities.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who will be responsible for what? (e.g., "I will collect the pictures for this column." "She'll edit the paragraphs." "He will describe our art project.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What past events will they narrate? (e.g., "Yesterday, we went to the Emberá Community.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What materials do they need? (e.g., "We need colorful photos." "What about adding more photos?")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must clarify their opinions and roles: "What do you think about adding more photos?" "I think this topic is meaningful." "Let's decide who will collect the pictures."</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use the target grammar (Future for plans, Past Simple for narration, Present Simple for needs/questions) and vocabulary to collaboratively plan their section. They must clarify opinions and roles, using appropriate language for teamwork.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then orally presents their plan to another group or to the class, describing their decisions and assigned tasks.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment during group presentations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally presents their newsletter section plan to the class. Students actively listen and use a simple checklist to evaluate their peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describes plans for newsletter content using 'will' (e.g., 'I will write the article.').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Narrates past events related to the trip (e.g., 'Yesterday, we went...').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks and answers questions in present simple ('What else do we need?').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifies personal opinions in group discussions ('What do you think about...?').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., article, pictures, topic, exciting, fun).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation with emphasis on main verbs in contractions (e.g., 'I'll <u>go</u>').</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or Quick Write):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you will do for our classroom newsletter?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you share your ideas clearly in your group? Yes/No, Why?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompts (Oral discussion):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of planning the newsletter did you find most interesting to talk about today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word or phrase you used today when talking about plans?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The group planning activity allowed for rich interaction and demonstrated students' ability to clarify opinions and plans. Students were actively using 'will' for plans and 'did' for past events. The pronunciation focus on verb emphasis in contractions was effective for some.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with spontaneously forming questions in the past simple. For the next lesson, I will ensure specific scaffolding for sentence construction when narrating past events and linking them to future plans. I will also incorporate more visual cues to support clarifying opinions (e.g., opinion cards). We need to transition from speaking about plans to writing them.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The speaking activities today about planning the newsletter will directly inform Lesson 4, where students will write their assigned articles or sections for the classroom newsletter, using the plans they discussed today.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>